      "level": 1,
      "content": "The type() Function"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "To verify everything is an object in Python, we can use the built-in type() function. For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1number = 10\n2print(type(number))"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "<class 'int'>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If we look at the output, we can see <class 'int'>. It means 10 is an object created from the int class."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's try a few more examples,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1numbers = [1, 4, 9, 16]\n2print(type(numbers))   # <class 'list'>   \n3\n4flag = True\n5print(type(True))   # <class 'bool'>\n6\n7def my_function():\n8    pass\n9\n10print(type(my_function))    # <class 'function'>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "As we can see, all these entities are created from a class, which means they are objects."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
